ios-eagl-es2.patch: add OpenGL ES 2.0 to the iOS host's EAGL backend, one EAGLContext per guest GL context.

Why: upstream gles-host-eagl.c creates a single ES 1.1 context for the whole process and ignores the guest's contexts
and share groups. CoreAnimation from iOS 4 on (in backboardd on iOS 6 and 7) creates API 2 contexts that run shaders,
which ES 1.1 cannot execute; with host GL off (the stub), iOS 7.1.2's SpringBoard crashes as soon as it creates a GL
context and never reaches the lock screen (docs/reports/m1-stage4-ios7.md). The pure EAGL build also no longer compiles
on the current main branch (the ES 1.1 headers lack GL_UNPACK_ROW_LENGTH and others, 20 errors): upstream has only
maintained the ANGLE branch since moving to the Mac in August.

How: the iOS EAGL build takes ANGLE's "one host ES context per guest context" path (on 10-21 upstream made n90ap
11D257 composite through ANGLE end to end on the Mac), replacing only context creation, switching and release with EAGL:
  - gles-host.c: a new macro GLES_HOST_ES_CONTEXTS (defined for ANGLE and iOS EAGL). The per-context code that used
    to be built only for CGL and ANGLE (gles_context_operation, the share group's root context, rectangle textures,
    APPLE_fence, submitting GLSL as is, no snapshots) all keys on it. On iOS both OpenGLES's ES1 and ES3 headers are
    included; the full-screen framebuffer goes through readback as with ANGLE.
  - New file gles-host-eagl.c.inc: the EAGL context structure and life cycle (guest API 2 creates ES 3.0, APIs 0 and
    1 create ES 1.1, one EAGLSharegroup per group shared from the root context). APPLE_fence is tracked here (iOS has
    no NV_fence to borrow; same semantics as the ANGLE version: every fence that was set counts as complete). It then
    includes the ES adaptation in gles-host-angle.c.inc.
  - gles-host-angle.c.inc: the EGL life cycle and the NV_fence parts are built only for ANGLE; BGRA textures use the
    RGBA internal format on iOS (APPLE_texture_format_BGRA8888). The ANGLE build behaves as before.
  - gles-host-eagl.c: now three plain C functions that create a context for an API, make it current and release it
    (still through the Objective-C runtime, see the file header), each call wrapped in an autorelease pool; drops the
    IOSurface color attachment that only worked for a single context.
  - Background: qemu_ios_set_foreground(false) (the app calls it in willResignActive) used to only flip a flag, so a
    GL call in progress on the vCPU thread could run past it; it now waits for that call to finish before returning
    (at most 1 second), and on returning to the foreground it prints how many calls were refused in the background.
Based on samhenrigold/qemu-ios b225694505; use together with ios-host-gles-option.patch (build with -Dhost_gles=enabled).

